  │ A "cares about" relation (subscription) │ No      │
  ├─────────────────────────────────────────┼─────────┤
  │ A delivery channel                      │ No      │
  └─────────────────────────────────────────┴─────────┘

  So this isn't one feature, it's a stack of them. Building notifications first would mean inventing a task model and a
  user model as a side effect of the notification design — which is how you get a task model shaped by notifications
  instead of the reverse.

  I want to understand the real situation before proposing anything, starting with the biggest fork.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether notifications are read out of a database by a server you control, or derived client-side from local
  state. This decides nearly everything downstream — whether "users" exist as accounts, whether a notification can reach
  someone who has the app closed, and whether any of this survives a browser cache clear.

  - [1mBackend exists elsewhere[0m — this repo is just a UI stub against a real API. Then notifications are mostly a client +
    subscription-model problem, and cross-device/offline delivery is on the table. Cheap path, if true.
  - [1mBackend needs building[0m — we're designing tasks, users, persistence, and events too. Largest scope, but everything
    becomes possible. Expensive to retrofit later.
  - [1mClient-only, no server[0m — localStorage, single browser, in-app only. Fast to build, but rules out email/push,
    multi-user, and multi-device permanently. Retrofitting a server later means redoing the data layer.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m if there's genuinely no backend, resist starting one for notifications' sake. Nail down what a task
  [3mis[0m first, then design notifications against it. But I need to know which world we're in.
